@@Tour
@Zortex

A note starts with its name (`@@Format tour`), any aliases (`@@Tour`) and
its tags (`@Zortex`). Then the body: headings, labels, lists, tasks and
links, written the way you would write them anyway. [Structure] links here.

# Headings and labels
A label ends with a colon and holds the lines under it.

Ingredients:
- Flour, water, salt
- A starter: see [Sourdough/#Starter]

Key: a label can also carry text on its line.

**A bold heading:**
- Sits between headings and labels in the outline

## A sub-heading
- Folds, the outline and the table of contents follow this structure.

# Inline
Words can be *italic*, **bold** or ***both***, `code`, or math: $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$.
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plain_text is a link, and so is a path: ./z/images/loop.svg.

$$
\int_0^1 x^2 \, dx = \frac{1}{3}
$$

```bash
echo "code blocks keep their language"
```

# Links
- [Sourdough] opens a note; [Sourdough/#Method/:Bake] a label inside it.
- [/#Headings and labels] stays in this note, and [/:Ingredients] finds the label.
- [/%colon] finds the first line containing the text.
- [P/#2.2 Bake a good sourdough loaf] reaches a project through the `P` alias.

